+# Agent Note: base bundle owns its shared service packages
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-08-14-base-bundle-owns-service-peers.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+The base bundle's plugin roster imports shared Service Definition and policy packages through peer dependencies. Those peers existed in the repository development installation but were absent from the `dsh-base` production dependency graph. A production-only CLI deployment could discover the roster through profile fallback healing, yet loading the plugins failed on 13 missing packages such as `dsh-fs`, `dsh-sandbox`, `dsh-scope`, `dsh-subprocess`, and `dsh-timeout`.
+
+The bundle already owns the provider and consumer roster that needs those packages. Leaving the shared packages to the repository root made the source workspace pass while the published composition remained incomplete.
+
+## Decision
+
+`@deepseek-ai/dsh-base` declares the 13 shared packages required by its shipped plugin roster as production dependencies. This follows the package rule that a composition host provides the peer packages used by its plugins and lets the profile fallback derive its links from the bundle dependency graph.
+
+The list includes shared definitions for filesystem, sandbox, shell, spill, subprocess, scope, telemetry, session title generation, subagent driving, output retention, atomic writes, anonymous identity, and timeout handling. Providers and consumers keep those packages as peers; only the composition host owns their installed instances.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+- **Adding the packages to the CLI manifest.** The CLI would duplicate bundle composition knowledge and would have to change whenever the base roster changed.
+- **Promoting peer dependencies inside every provider to regular dependencies.** This risks separate Cordis service instances and changes package ownership across the repository instead of fixing the composition host.
+- **Deploying development dependencies.** This hides incomplete production manifests, ships tests and build tools, and prevents production-only installation from being verified.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Production deployment includes 13 small workspace packages that were already required at runtime. The base bundle manifest is longer, but its dependency graph now fully represents the default composition and works for every host that installs the bundle, not only the desktop wrapper.
+
+## Testing
+
+`packages/bundle/base/tests/production-dependencies.spec.ts` pins the required shared-package set. The desktop assembled-runtime test starts the production deployment with no repository `node_modules` or system Node available and requires Web profile startup to complete.
